Build a C++17 program that determines whether a short input string can be generated by a context-free grammar (CFG). The program must search leftmost derivations, validate its grammar and input, show one successful derivation when it accepts, and distinguish a proven rejection from a search that reaches a safety bound. 

Overall, you might spend about two to four hours in total. This can vary, so it is a good idea to set aside a few hours to work through it at a comfortable pace. Review the 1.5 Information: Group Project Work page to set your team up for success. 

## Background
A context-free grammar has four parts:

For this project:

a finite set of variables (nonterminals);
a finite set of terminals;
a finite set of productions; and
a start variable.
Each variable is one uppercase character, and each terminal is one non-uppercase character.
A production body is a string of variables and terminals with no spaces.
Use eps in a displayed grammar to represent an empty production body. eps is notation for the empty string; it is not an input character. Required test strings contain at most 12 characters.

## Required Outcomes

Your program must return one of five outcomes:

Accepted
Rejected
Inconclusive
Invalid grammar
Invalid input
Safety-bound requirements:

Do not report Rejected merely because a safety limit was reached.
If a possible branch is cut off by a search limit and no accepting derivation is found, report Inconclusive.
Report Rejected only when the search proves that no derivation generates the input.

## Implementation Requirements

1. Represent the grammar's variables, terminals, production alternatives, and start variable.

2. Validate the grammar and the input before searching.

3. Use a queue to perform a bounded breadth-first search over leftmost derivations.

4. Use a visited set so the same sentential form is not processed repeatedly.

5. Apply the sound pruning rules and safety limits specified in the tutorial.
6. Print one leftmost derivation when an input is accepted.
7. Pass every case in the required test-case document.
Use the exact required outcome labels shown above.
## Result Reporting
Accepted: print one successful leftmost derivation.
Rejected, Inconclusive, Invalid grammar, or Invalid input: print a short reason.
Validate the grammar and input before searching.
Do not treat reaching a safety limit as rejection.
Required Testing
Run every case in the required test-case document.
Compile with C++17 and warnings enabled.
Input and Output Details

Input:
a context-free grammar using the bounded format in the tutorial; and
a short input string to test.
Output:
one of the five required outcomes.
Additional output:

one successful leftmost derivation for an accepted input; or
a short reason for a rejected, inconclusive, or invalid result.
